It seems that Muon-Hyperball can be replaced with Muon-SODA, as both eliminate weight decay and SODA requires no extra hyperparameters.

@tonysilveti explains that SODA shows Muon, Lion, NAdam, and others are all special cases of Optimistic Dual Averaging. A simple wrapper around any base optimizer can replace weight decay tuning with a principled 1/(k+2) schedule, without introducing new hyperparameters. The practical results appear promising.

The linked arXiv paper discusses how Optimistic Dual Averaging unifies modern optimizers, providing a theoretical foundation for these methods.

